  "result": "**Tool calling, in short:** the model can't *do* anything itself. It can only write out a request. Your program carries out the request and reports back.\n\n**Analogy: an NFL offensive coordinator**\n- The coordinator (the model) sits in the booth with a menu of plays (the tools). He never touches the ball.\n- He picks a play and radios it in: \"Run 22 Zone, left side.\" That's the tool call. It's only text, a request.\n- The players (your program) run the play on the field.\n- The result comes back: \"Gained 4 yards, 2nd and 6.\" The coordinator reads it and calls the next play.\n- This repeats until the drive ends in a score or a punt. That's the loop stopping.\n\n**Where it maps**\n- **Tool:** a play on the menu. It has a name and a description so he knows when to call it.\n- **Tool call:** the radioed play call.\n- **Tool result:** the report from the field.\n\n**Where it breaks:** a coordinator remembers the whole game. The model only knows what's written in the text it's handed each turn. Your program has to pass the earlier calls and results back in every time. That's the next topic, context.\n\n**Quick check:** if the coordinator calls a play, who actually executes it, and what does he see afterward?",
